The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements concerning the situation in South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2206 (2015), 2428 (2018), 2471 (2019), 2521 (2020), 2577 (2021), 2633 (2022), 2683 (2023), 2731 (2024), and 2779 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of the Republic of South Sudan, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness, and regional cooperation,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating political, security, and humanitarian situation in South Sudan, including the ongoing violence, armed clashes, human rights violations and abuses, including widespread conflict-related sexual violence, recruitment and use of children, and targeted killings, as well as violations of international humanitarian law,

Expressing deep concern at the persistent delays in the implementation of the 2018 Revitalized Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in the Republic of South Sudan (the Revitalized Agreement) and the Roadmap, stressing that the peace process requires the genuine and urgent political commitment of all parties, and reiterating that the Revitalized Agreement remains the only viable framework to achieve sustainable peace and stability in South Sudan,

Reiterating its serious concern regarding the failure to hold credible, free, fair, and peaceful elections as previously scheduled, and taking note of the decision to extend the transitional period until December 2026, while underscoring that the Revitalized Transitional Government of National Unity must urgently disburse necessary funding and demonstrate tangible political will to establish the legal, constitutional, and security architecture required for credible elections,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks directed against civilians, humanitarian personnel, and United Nations personnel, and expressing alarm at the continuing obstructions to humanitarian operations, including excessive taxation, unlawful fees, bureaucratic impediments, and direct attacks, which severely exacerbate the catastrophic humanitarian conditions affecting more than 9 million people,

Expressing grave concern over the spillover effects of the ongoing conflict in Sudan, including the massive influx of returnees and refugees, disruptions to critical cross-border trade and pipeline infrastructure, and the heightened risk of regional destabilization and illicit arms flows across borders,

Reiterating that the illicit transfer, destabilizing accumulation, and misuse of small arms and light weapons fuel and prolong conflict, undermine the rule of law, and exacerbate violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law,

Emphasizing that the sanctions measures renewed by this resolution are designed to prevent the fueling of conflict and promote accountability, and are not intended to impede the implementation of the peace agreement or have negative humanitarian consequences for the civilian population,

Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts on South Sudan (S/2025/290) and the findings and recommendations contained therein,

Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to pose a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the United Nations Charter,

1. Decides to renew until 31 May 2026 the measures on arms imposed by paragraph 4 of resolution 2428 (2018), and reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 5 and 6 of resolution 2428 (2018) and paragraph 2 of resolution 2683 (2023);

2. Decides to renew until 31 May 2026 the travel and financial measures imposed by paragraphs 9 and 12 of resolution 2206 (2015), and reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 10, 11, 13, 14, and 15 of resolution 2206 (2015), and paragraphs 13, 14, 15, and 16 of resolution 2428 (2018);

3. Reiterates its readiness to review the measures renewed in this resolution, including through the modification, suspension, or progressive lifting of the arms embargo, in the light of progress achieved on the key benchmarks established in paragraph 2 of resolution 2577 (2021), specifically:
(a) The completion of the Strategic Defence and Security Review process;
(b) The formation and deployment of the Necessary Unified Forces and their unified command structure;
(c) The progress made on disarmament, demobilization, and reintegration (DDR);
(d) The progress made on properly managing and securing existing arms and ammunition stockpiles;
(e) The implementation of the Joint Action Plan for the Armed Forces on addressing Conflict-Related Sexual Violence;

4. Calls upon the Revitalized Transitional Government of National Unity to make concrete and expedited progress on the benchmarks outlined in paragraph 3, and to provide adequate domestic resources to support their implementation;

5. Requests the South Sudan authorities to report, no later than 15 April 2026, to the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2206 (2015) concerning South Sudan (the Committee) on the progress achieved on the key benchmarks;

6. Requests the Secretary-General, in close consultation with the United Nations Mission in South Sudan (UNMISS) and the Panel of Experts, to conduct an assessment of progress achieved on the key benchmarks by 30 April 2026;

7. Decides to extend until 1 July 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts as set out in paragraph 19 of resolution 2428 (2018), and decides that the Panel of Experts shall provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, an interim report by 1 December 2025, a final report by 1 May 2026, and monthly updates to the Committee;

8. Urges all Member States, relevant United Nations bodies, and other interested parties to cooperate fully with the Committee and the Panel of Experts, in particular by supplying any information at their disposal concerning the implementation of the measures imposed by this resolution;

9. Strongly condemns the harassment, obstruction, and denial of access encountered by the Panel of Experts in the execution of its mandate, and insists that the South Sudan authorities grant the Panel unhindered access and freedom of movement throughout the country;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements concerning the situation in South Sudan, in particular resolutions 2206 (2015), 2428 (2018), 2471 (2019), 2521 (2020), 2577 (2021), 2633 (2022), 2683 (2023), 2731 (2024), and 2779 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity, and national unity of the Republic of South Sudan, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness, and regional cooperation,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating political, security, and humanitarian situation in South Sudan, including the ongoing violence, armed clashes, human rights violations and abuses, including widespread conflict-related sexual violence, recruitment and use of children, and targeted killings, as well as violations of international humanitarian law, and stressing the urgent need to end impunity and hold accountable all those responsible for such violations,

Expressing deep concern at the persistent delays in the implementation of the 2018 Revitalized Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict in the Republic of South Sudan (the Revitalized Agreement) and the Roadmap, welcoming the deployment of the first batch of the Necessary Unified Forces while recognizing the logistical and resource constraints faced by the Transitional Government, stressing that the peace process requires the genuine and urgent political commitment of all parties, reiterating that the Revitalized Agreement remains the only viable framework to achieve sustainable peace and stability in South Sudan, and emphasizing the critical importance of advancing transitional justice and accountability mechanisms under Chapter V of the Revitalized Agreement, including the Hybrid Court for South Sudan, to end impunity and achieve sustainable reconciliation,

Reiterating its serious concern regarding the failure to hold credible, free, fair, and peaceful elections as previously scheduled, and taking note of the decision to extend the transitional period until December 2026, while underscoring that the Revitalized Transitional Government of National Unity must urgently disburse necessary funding and demonstrate tangible political will to establish the legal, constitutional, and security architecture required for credible elections, with the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and the protection of civic and political space,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks directed against civilians, humanitarian personnel, and United Nations personnel, recalling that attacks intentionally directed against humanitarian personnel and peacekeepers may constitute war crimes, expressing alarm at the continuing obstructions to humanitarian operations, including excessive taxation, unlawful fees, bureaucratic impediments, and direct attacks, which severely exacerbate the catastrophic humanitarian conditions affecting more than 9 million people, emphasizing the urgent need to end impunity and ensure accountability for all attacks against humanitarian workers and United Nations personnel, and recalling that all parties must respect their obligations under international humanitarian law, including with regard to ensuring the safety of humanitarian personnel and facilitating full, safe, rapid, and unhindered humanitarian access,

Expressing grave concern over the spillover effects of the ongoing conflict in Sudan, including the massive influx of returnees and refugees, disruptions to critical cross-border trade and pipeline infrastructure, including the severe economic and fiscal strain resulting from the recent shutdown of the Greater Nile Oil Pipeline which constrains the Government's capacity to deliver essential services and implement transitional arrangements, and the heightened risk of regional destabilization and illicit arms flows across borders,

Taking note of the calls by the African Union and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) regarding the arms embargo, and emphasizing the need for the international community to assist the transitional government in building state capacity, particularly in completing security sector reform and deploying the Necessary Unified Forces,

Reiterating that the illicit transfer, destabilizing accumulation, and misuse of small arms and light weapons fuel and prolong conflict, undermine the rule of law, and exacerbate violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law,

Emphasizing that the sanctions measures renewed by this resolution are designed to prevent the fueling of conflict and promote accountability, and are not intended to impede the implementation of the peace agreement or have negative humanitarian consequences for the civilian population, emphasizing that sanctions measures should be calibrated and regularly reviewed to ensure they do not impede national state-building efforts, and recalling resolution 2664 (2022) regarding humanitarian exemptions,

Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts on South Sudan (S/2025/290) and the findings and recommendations contained therein,

Determining that the situation in South Sudan continues to pose a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the United Nations Charter,

1. Decides to renew until 31 May 2026 the measures on arms imposed by paragraph 4 of resolution 2428 (2018), and reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 5 and 6 of resolution 2428 (2018) and paragraph 2 of resolution 2683 (2023), and further decides that these measures shall not apply to the supply, sale, or transfer of non-lethal military equipment, technical assistance, or training intended solely for the support of or use by the Necessary Unified Forces;

